WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.020
Film Courage: It sounds like Left Brain (i.e.,
Conceptual Writers) have a lot of trouble

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.020
letting go.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:11.350
They are so formulaic and they’re so much
about making sure that something makes sense,

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.940
whereas with these Right Brain people, they
are all over the place?

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.049
They are just too easily led.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:20.300
So it sounds like you do exercises to help
the Left Brain people let go and the Right

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.910
Brain people reign themselves in?

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.759
Corey Mandell:&nbsp; So there is another video
I can send people, and again you can email

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:26.420
my assistant which is lisa@coreymandell.net.&nbsp;

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:31.460
And it’s this neurologist and she’ll really
break down the conceptual and intuitive parts

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.460
of the brain.&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.510
And literally what one part is good at and
what the other part is good at, but they cant

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.960
talk to each other because they have a different
processor like in the old days with PCs and

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:41.960
Apple.&nbsp;

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.379
So like you have a dream which is a very intuitive
experience, non-linear, non-casual, but it’s

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:48.449
really evocative.&nbsp;

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:51.789
And then you try to tell someone about the
dream which is a conceptual exercise.&nbsp;

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:55.010
And the best you can get them do is kind of
go “Oh?

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.199
I see how you had a really interesting dream!”&nbsp;

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:02.800
But they don’t necessarily it that way.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.800
The hardest part is turning off your home
base.&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.960
And then when I can get you over to this other
side I can give you because everyone knows

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.700
about this 10,000 hours to become an expert
at something.&nbsp;

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.090
Well even Malcolm Gladwell has said that the
10,000 hours is the wrong thing to focus on.&nbsp;

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.580
There’s nothing magical about 10,000.&nbsp;

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.810
What he said it was 10,000 hours of dedicated
practice.&nbsp;

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.090
Everyone latches onto 10,000 but everyone
forgets dedicated practice.&nbsp;

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.370
Dedicated practice is the way people become
an expert at something, sure they’ve got

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.890
to put a lot of time, energy and passion into
something.&nbsp;

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.180
But it’s what they do with their training.&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.060
It’s how they train.&nbsp;

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.310
And so if you think about…I trained at Second
City improv.&nbsp;

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.670
They don’t let you on stage for a year and
a half.&nbsp;

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:54.610
Because you’re doing specific exercises
everyday to learn certain key skill sets,

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:58.380
that ultimately they’ll integrate together,
then they’ll put you on stage.&nbsp;

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.170
Same thing with Juilliard and acting.&nbsp;

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.110
Same thing if you’re a professional athlete.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:10.140
&nbsp; It’s about specific exercises to train
yourself to develop the key skill sets and

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.140
integrate.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:17.030
(And I’m sure we will talk about this at
some point) I was a working studio writer

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:25.450
for 11 years and while I did that, I taught
once a year at UCLA to give back and for fun.&nbsp;

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:31.270
And then over the last 6 years I’ve been
building a teaching business and teaching

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:33.090
and working with writers.&nbsp;

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:41.140
And I have a lot of friends and I know a lot
of people who are very successful writers.&nbsp;

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:47.740
And almost all of them either figured stuff
out themselves which takes about 10 to 12

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.670
years for most people.&nbsp;

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.390
Or they had someone help them.&nbsp;

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:57.010
And there are two kinds of people that help
them, a manager or they get to be a writer’s

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:02.180
assistant staffed on a TV show following a
show runner and that show runner’s process.&nbsp;

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.430
I know someone who worked on BREAKING BAD
and with Vince Gilligan for three years.&nbsp;

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.840
And Vince Gilligan taught her how to change
her process.&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.770
In fact the greatest story…she was very
intuitive and she got onto BREAKING BAD.&nbsp;

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:20.069
And BREAKING BAD…what happened (in the beginning
of the season), Vince Gilligan will ask all

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:25.270
the writers in the room to come up with certain
ideas and if he likes the idea, he writes

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.430
it on an index card and it goes on the board.&nbsp;

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.470
And ultimately there are all these ideas on
the board that they are going to work with

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.250
to develop the season.&nbsp;

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.220
Well, if you’re a new writer, you really
want to get a lot of ideas on the board and

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:41.650
if none of your ideas get on the board, that
doesn’t look so good.&nbsp;

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.099
Well his rule was his idea had to fit on the
card.

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:49.069
&nbsp; So if you start pitching an idea and he’s
writing and he runs out of room, it may be

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:52.750
a great idea but he ran out of room on the
card, it doesn’t go on the card.&nbsp;

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.310
And she’s like “I couldn’t get my ideas
out succinctly enough.”&nbsp;

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.970
She said “I don’t know how to speak in
index card.”&nbsp;

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.500
And so she had to learn (and this is one of
the things I train intuitive writers to do)

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:10.280
is how to take what they see and feel and
how to communicate it in a very concise way.&nbsp;

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.250
And so that’s just one example.&nbsp;

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.680
I’m sure she learned a million things from
working with someone like Vince Gilligan.&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.000
But that is just one example.&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.880
She learned how to be very concise on how
she thought and felt and pitched.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:29.240
So the way that most writers get there, they
worked in TV for several years with a really

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.240
great show runner.&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.620
And through that process you learn these skill
sets and you adapt and you develop or you

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.159
just get pushed out.

00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:43.439
Or there’s a manager who will work intently
with you because the reality is most writers

00:04:43.439 --> 00:04:47.100
they have certain strengths, certain weaknesses
and certain blind spots.&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.340
Blind spots are weaknesses you don’t know
you have.&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.569
The key is the figure out A) what your blindspots
are so at least they’re known weakness.&nbsp;

00:04:55.569 --> 00:04:57.990
Then how do you turn those weaknesses into
strengths?&nbsp;

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.110
That’s where the dedicated practice comes
in.&nbsp;

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.639
And it’s very difficult to do this yourself.&nbsp;

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:10.659
So a manager (who really knows what they’re
doing) they will develop a writer…they’ll

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:15.460
take a writer who’s really good but not
exceptional and they will train that writer

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.740
over one, two or three years to do this.&nbsp;

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:22.039
And then, when that writer can write at that
level, they’ll go out with the script pretending

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:24.449
that it was the first thing the writer ever
wrote.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:27.750
By definition when you sell something, that’s
the first script you ever wrote.&nbsp;

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.999
You never talk about the rest of it.

00:05:29.999 --> 00:05:36.740
&nbsp; So the writers that I know, who have careers
(especially the best careers), generally speaking

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:41.930
either they were trained by show runners (because
they were able to work in TV early on) or

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.139
they were trained by managers.

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:45.849
&nbsp; Very few of them were self-trained.&nbsp;

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:46.849
It’s possible.&nbsp;

00:05:46.849 --> 00:05:51.229
I know some examples but it took a decade
and they’re rare that they got there.

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:54.940
&nbsp; Well, the reality is there are a lot of
writers who don’t live in Los Angeles so

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.009
they can’t work on the show.&nbsp;

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:00.210
And even if you live in Los Angeles and you’re
like “Okay, I’ll work on a show for training.&nbsp;

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.210
Where do I sign up?”&nbsp;

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:02.210
It’s not that easy.&nbsp;

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.349
It’s very competitive.&nbsp;

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:09.060
And the thing about a manager is they’re
so busy servicing their clients no one wants

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.119
to do development anymore so the manager has
to really work with their top writers and

00:06:14.119 --> 00:06:17.710
really help them develop their material and
they don’t have time to work with their

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.039
new writers or emerging writers.&nbsp;

00:06:20.039 --> 00:06:24.710
So writers are like “I have a manager but
they won’t spend a lot of time helping me.”&nbsp;

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.949
And there are all these managers saying “God,
I really wish I could be helping these writers

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:29.669
but I don’t have time.”

00:06:29.669 --> 00:06:33.660
&nbsp; So it’s really hard to find someone to
do that kind of training.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.689
So that’s when I used to teach at UCLA for
a long time and I was…someone challenged

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:43.599
me like “Can you develop a training program
to train writers like this, if they don’t

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.219
have a manager who can do this for them.&nbsp;

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:49.559
If they are not yet able to get on a TV show
or they don’t want to or they don’t live

00:06:49.559 --> 00:06:51.650
in LA.&nbsp;

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.139
Is there another way they can learn these
skill sets?”&nbsp;

00:06:54.139 --> 00:07:01.160
Because a lot of the programs out there, they
are just telling people to take your writing

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.710
and put it in this paradigm.&nbsp;

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:07.849
But what I did, I wanted to create a series
of workshops that could teach these new skills.&nbsp;

00:07:07.849 --> 00:07:09.219
So that is where this all came from.

00:07:09.219 --> 00:07:09.509
